A||||2||Patrick||Hot start problems||||4||
Z||000000||Patrick||06-20-2000||04:04 PM||||<BR>Time flies, but a couple of months ago I was pulling my hair out over the recurring <B><I>hot start</I></B> problem my Fiero had. In this thread-  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002700.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002700.html</A>  I had stated the following-<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>One thing that I don't think anyone has mentioned is whether the particular thermostat installed has any relevance to the hot start problem. As far as I know, there are two (stock) thermostats available for the 2.5L, 180 degrees and 195. (It might be the same for the 2.8L) Has anyone had any experience regarding whether or not it has made a difference which thermostat they had installed?<BR> <BR>It seems to me that if an engine has a problem starting when it's hot, yet will start fine after the trunk has been open for five minutes, then it might make enough difference simply by replacing a 195 thermostat with a 180 unit. That 15 degree difference might make a world of difference.<BR> <BR>I had changed mine from 180 to 195 three years ago when I bought my Fiero because I had previously used 195 degree thermostats in all my older high performance Chevys. However, I always felt that the Fiero didn't perform as well with this higher temperature thermostat. I believe Ogre has commented on this subject as well. Maybe it's been a HUGE mistake having the 195 thermostat installed. Not only does it appear to negatively affect performance, but it's possible that it also contributes to hot start problems.<BR> <BR>I'm going to change the thermostat back to 180 and I'll report any improvement.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, I'm finally reporting back on this. May I have a drum roll please...<P><B>IT WORKED !!!</B><P>No more hot start problems... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000001||Gokart||06-20-2000||08:16 PM||||Good news Patrick! I'll be changing mine to a 180 this week anyway with the warmer weather coming. I'm wondering if it's a safe thermostat to use year round or should I go back to 195 for the winter?||||reg||14
Z||000002||Patrick||06-21-2000||01:56 AM||||<BR>Yeah, I was rather pleased Marc. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Leaving the 180 in all year round depends on the winters you get back there. Here on the west coast it's usually so mild in the winter that I'm going to leave that sucker in all year. It rarely gets below freezing here, so I shouldn't have any trouble. You'd probably be able to tell if the 180 was too low in the winter if the air from your heater felt luke-warm rather than hot. Someone else from a colder climate may beg to differ with me though.||||reg||14
